.\" Manual page for git-new-worktree.
.\" Hand-written roff. There is no generator; edit this file directly.
.TH GIT\-NEW\-WORKTREE 1 "2026-08-18" "git-refresh" "Git Extras"
.
.SH NAME
git\-new\-worktree \- add a worktree beside the others, creating the
branch if it does not exist
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git new\-worktree
.RB [ \-f ]
.I branch
.RI [ base ]
.br
.B git new\-worktree
.B \-h
.PP
Also installed as
.BR git\-new\-worktree ,
and reachable as
.B git new\-worktree
without an alias: git looks for a
.B git\-new\-worktree
in
.B PATH
before it consults aliases.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git new\-worktree
adds a worktree for
.I branch
next to the repository's other worktrees, then prints its path. It replaces the
usual pair of "new branch" and "check out existing branch" helpers: which of the
two you wanted is decided by whether the branch already exists.
.PP
Worktrees are siblings of the bare repository, one directory per branch, named
after the branch:
.PP
.EX
myrepo/
  .git/              the bare repository
  main/              a worktree
  add\-billing\-ui/    a worktree
.EE
.PP
The layout is derived from the git common directory rather than from the current
checkout, so it is right both before and after a conversion.
.PP
Unless the worktree already exists, the run fetches every remote first, and
fast\-forwards origin's default branch from
.BR upstream 's
when an
.B upstream
remote exists. If the two have genuinely diverged it says so and branches from
origin as\-is.
.PP
What happens then depends on
.IR branch :
.IP \(bu 3
The worktree directory already exists: its path is printed and the command
exits 0. See
.B "Already checked out"
below.
.IP \(bu 3
The branch exists locally: it is checked out into the new worktree. A bare clone
lands every branch in
.B refs/heads
with no upstream, so if the branch has none and
.IR origin/ branch
exists, the upstream is set.
.IP \(bu 3
The branch exists on origin but not locally: it is created with
.B \-\-track
against
.IR origin/ branch.
.IP \(bu 3
Neither: the branch is created from
.I base
with
.BR \-\-no\-track ,
and
.IB branch. <name> .base
is recorded. See
.B "Recording the base"
below.
.
.SS Recording the base
When \(em and only when \(em this command creates a branch, it writes the branch
point into git config:
.PP
.EX
git config branch.<branch>.base <base>
.EE
.PP
.I base
is stored without any
.B origin/
prefix. This is the one moment the branch point is known for certain, and it is
what
.BR git\-refresh (1)
reads to find the right base before a pull request exists. Without it, a branch
has no base until its pull request is opened and
.B "git refresh \-\-all"
has nothing to rebase it onto \(em it reports the branch as
.B "no base"
and leaves it alone. The key is plain git config and is safe to set or correct
by hand.
.PP
The new branch is created
.B \-\-no\-track
on purpose. Branching from
.IR origin/ <default>
would otherwise leave that as the new branch's upstream, which is the wrong
branch: every branch here wants
.IR origin/ <its\ own\ name>,
set by the first
.BR "git push \-u" .
Setting
.B push.autoSetupRemote
to
.B true
makes a plain
.B git push
do that for you.
.
.SS Converting an existing clone
A plain clone has no sibling worktrees to add to: its one checkout sits where
the worktrees belong. Run
.B git new\-worktree
inside one and it offers to convert the clone in place \(em
.B .git
becomes bare
.RB ( core.bare
is set
.BR true )
and the current checkout moves to
.IR repo / <current\-branch>/.
.PP
The conversion deletes the old checkout and lets
.B "git worktree add"
lay it down again, so anything git is not tracking would not come back. It
therefore
.B requires a pristine checkout:
no modified files, nothing untracked, nothing ignored, as judged by
.BR "git status \-\-porcelain \-\-ignored" .
Anything left over is refused with the
.B "git clean \-ndx"
and
.B "git clean \-fdx"
commands to review and remove it.
.PP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
skips the interactive prompt.
.B It does not skip the pristine check.
There is no flag that does; clean the checkout instead. Conversion is also
refused from a linked worktree of a non\-bare clone (convert from the main
worktree), from a clone that already has linked worktrees (convert that by
hand), from a detached HEAD, and on a non\-interactive standard input without
.BR \-\-force .
.
.SS Already checked out
If the worktree directory for
.I branch
already exists and holds that branch, the path is printed and the command exits
.BR 0 ,
before the fetch, rather than failing over a worktree that is exactly what was
asked for. Whoever typed
.B "gwt \fIbranch\fP"
wants to be in it, and there is nothing to create. A directory of that name
holding some other branch is an error.
.PP
This is what makes
.B gwt
usable as one command for both "make me this branch" and "take me to this
branch".
.
.SS Output streams
The worktree path, and nothing else, goes to
.BR stdout .
Every message, prompt and passed\-through git command output goes to
.BR stderr .
.PP
That split exists because a script cannot change its caller's directory, so the
.B cd
has to happen in your shell. The
.B gwt
shell function \(em installed as
.I share/git\-refresh/gwt.sh
\(em captures stdout and nothing else:
.PP
.EX
gwt() {
    local d
    d=$(git new\-worktree "$@") || return
    cd "$d"
}
.EE
.PP
Anything printed on stdout that is not the path would break that
.BR cd ,
which is why the messages are on stderr even when they are good news.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I branch
The branch to add a worktree for. Checked out if it already exists locally,
tracked if it exists on origin, otherwise created.
.TP
.I base
Branch point for a new branch. Defaults to origin's default branch. A bare name
is looked up under origin first, so
.B main
means
.B origin/main
where that exists, falling back to any other ref of that name. Ignored when the
branch already exists.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Convert a plain clone to the worktree layout without asking. Skips the prompt,
not the pristine\-checkout check.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. The next arguments are
.I branch
and
.IR base .
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE ", " GIT_INDEX_FILE ", " GIT_PREFIX
Unset before anything else runs, so that the git alias entry point cannot
retarget the git calls in the run.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB repo /.git
The bare repository. Worktrees are its siblings,
.IB repo / <branch>\fR.
.TP
.I $PREFIX/share/git\-refresh/gwt.sh
The
.B gwt
shell function. Source it from your shell rc.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The worktree exists and its path was printed \(em whether this run created it or
found it already there.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error: not in a repository, no such base, more than a branch and a base
given, a directory in the way that is not a worktree for this branch, a
conversion declined or refused, or a checkout that is not pristine.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, or no branch given.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
New branch off the default branch, with the base recorded, and cd'd into it:
.PP
.EX
gwt my\-feature
.EE
.PP
New branch stacked on another, so
.B git refresh
rebases it onto that one:
.PP
.EX
gwt add\-billing\-ui add\-billing\-writes
.EE
.PP
Check out a branch that already exists on origin:
.PP
.EX
gwt colleagues\-branch
.EE
.PP
Without the shell function, taking the path yourself:
.PP
.EX
cd "$(git new\-worktree my\-feature)"
.EE
.PP
Convert a plain clone to the worktree layout, no prompt:
.PP
.EX
cd ~/some\-plain\-clone
git clean \-fdx
git new\-worktree \-f my\-feature
.EE
.PP
Correct a recorded base by hand:
.PP
.EX
git config branch.my\-feature.base add\-billing\-writes
.EE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-refresh (1),
.BR git\-clone\-for\-worktrees (1),
.BR git\-worktree (1),
.BR git\-rebase (1),
.BR git\-push (1)
.
